Constructors in C++
A constructor is a special member function of a class that is automatically called when an object is created. It is used to initialize objects.

Features of a constructor:
- Its name is the same as the class name.
- It has no return type, not even void.
- It is called automatically when an object is created.

Types of constructors:

1. Default constructor
A constructor that takes no arguments is called a default constructor.
class Student {
public:
    Student() {
        cout << "Default constructor";
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s;
}


Output:
Default constructor

Explanation: When object s is created, the constructor runs automatically without taking any arguments.

2. Parameterized constructor

A constructor that takes one or more arguments is called a parameterized constructor.
class Student {
public:
    int age;

    Student(int a) {
        age = a;
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s(18);
    cout << s.age;
}


Output:
18

Explanation: The value 18 is passed to the constructor and stored in age.

3. Copy constructor

A copy constructor creates a new object by initializing it with an existing object of the same class.
class Student {
public:
    int age;

    Student(int a) {
        age = a;
    }

    Student(const Student &s) {
        age = s.age;
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s1(18);
    Student s2(s1);
    cout << s2.age;
}


Output:
18

Explanation: The object s2 copies the age value from the existing object s1.

Can we overload a constructor?
Yes, constructors can be overloaded in C++.

Constructor overloading means defining multiple constructors in the same class with different parameter lists. They can differ in the number or types of parameters.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    int age;

    Student() {
        age = 0;
    }

    Student(int a) {
        age = a;
    }

    Student(int a, int b) {
        age = a + b;
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s1;
    Student s2(18);
    Student s3(10, 20);

    cout << s1.age << endl;
    cout << s2.age << endl;
    cout << s3.age;
}


Output:
0
18
30
